{
  "dom_id": "step-q94464",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ is positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
